.TH CS 4
.SH NAME
cs \- the connection server: a dial string becomes an address
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cs
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Cs
posts
.B /srv/cs
and serves one file,
.BR cs ,
which
.IR init (8)
mounts after the stack's tree at
.BR /net .
A program writes a dial string of the form
.IB proto ! host ! service
and reads back the clone file to open and the far end to connect to:
.IP
.EX
% echo tcp!fs!9fs > /net/cs
/net/tcp/clone 10.0.2.15!564
.EE
.PP
.I Proto
is
.BR tcp ,
.B udp
or
.BR icmp .
A
.I host
that is already a dotted address is used as it stands;
.B *
is this machine, read from
.BR /net/local ;
any other name is looked up in
.B /lib/ndb/local
as a
.B sys=
record's
.BR ip ,
and failing that is asked of
.IR dns (4).
A
.I service
that is already a number is used as it stands; any other name is the
port the database gives it under the protocol, as
.BR tcp=9fs\ port=564 .
A name neither the database nor the network knows fails the write with
.BR ENOENT .
.PP
The answer belongs to the descriptor that asked, is read whole whatever
the offset, and is forgotten once read: a second read answers nothing.
Two programs dialling at once do not see each other's answer.
.PP
A
.B Tremove
of the file stops the server.
.SH SOURCE
.B servers/cs/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR dns (4),
.IR net (2),
.IR ping (1),
.IR netecho (1)
.SH BUGS
The database is read once, at start.
A dial string names one address; there is no list to walk when the
first does not answer.
